---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Attentive vs Mailchimp for Shopify: SMS Depth or Email Familiarity?';
const description = 'A focused comparison of Attentive and Mailchimp for Shopify email and SMS, consent, lifecycle workflows, pricing, and operating fit.';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can Attentive replace Mailchimp entirely?', a: 'Usually not. Attentive is SMS-first, while Mailchimp provides email campaigns, journeys, landing pages, and newsletter workflows. Decide whether SMS specialization or email breadth is the binding requirement.' },
  { q: 'Should a Shopify store use both?', a: 'It can, when email and SMS have separate owners and the team synchronizes consent, purchases, suppression, and quiet windows. Test the same customer journey across both before scaling.' },
  { q: 'How should pricing be compared?', a: 'Model Mailchimp contacts and SMS costs against Attentive contract minimums, message volume, implementation, legal review, support, and the revenue required to make SMS incremental.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="py-12 md:py-20"><div class="container max-w-4xl">
    <a href="/compare" class="text-sm text-muted inline-block mb-6">← All comparisons</a>
    <header class="mb-10"><p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary mb-3">Two-product Shopify comparison</p><h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">{title}</h1><p class="text-xl text-muted">Attentive and Mailchimp can both reach Shopify customers, but they are built around different channel priorities. This page compares only those two products.</p></header>
    <section class="rounded-xl border border-primary/20 p-6 mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Short answer</h2><p>Choose Attentive when consented SMS is a major revenue channel with dedicated legal, lifecycle, and conversational ownership. Choose Mailchimp when email campaigns, newsletters, and approachable customer journeys matter more than SMS depth.</p><p class="mt-3">The right answer depends on the store’s constraint. A text-first team should test opt-in quality, quiet hours, replies, compliance review, and mobile recovery. An email-first team should test segmentation, purchase suppression, content production, reporting, and total contact cost.</p></section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Decision snapshot</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Attentive</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best fit</td><td>Enterprise SMS and conversational retention</td><td>Email campaigns and approachable journeys</td></tr><tr><td>Core strength</td><td>SMS consent, personalization, governance, and scale</td><td>Email editor, newsletters, audiences, and broad marketing workflows</td></tr><tr><td>Main trade-off</td><td>Contract and operational commitment can be substantial</td><td>SMS and advanced commerce depth may be limited for complex programs</td></tr><tr><td>Pricing question</td><td>What volume and attributed margin justify the commitment?</td><td>How do contacts, sends, features, and SMS credits scale?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Attentive: when it fits</h2><p>Attentive is a strong candidate when SMS is not an add-on but a primary retention channel. Dedicated keyword, opt-in, quiet-hour, review, and conversational workflows can matter for a mobile-first catalog with enough volume to support the operating and commercial commitment.</p><p class="mt-3">Pilot one consented cart or launch journey with legal review, purchase suppression, reply handling, frequency caps, and a holdout. Measure incremental margin, opt-outs, support load, and deliverability—not only attributed revenue from recipients who were already likely to buy.</p>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Mailchimp: when it fits</h2><p>Mailchimp is a practical fit when the team needs a familiar email editor, newsletters, audience management, and basic customer journeys without adopting an enterprise SMS operating model. It can be enough for stores whose primary work is email education, launches, and moderate lifecycle automation.</p><p class="mt-3">Validate Shopify customer and product synchronization, purchase and refund exits, consent fields, audience growth, SMS add-on costs, and reporting. Keep a clear line between promotional messages and operational notices, especially when phone data originated from checkout or shipping communication.</p>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Controlled pilot</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Attentive</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry</td><td>Verified SMS opt-in, cart, keyword, or campaign segment</td><td>Email opt-in, Shopify customer, product, or audience event</td></tr><tr><td>Exit</td><td>Purchase, opt-out, quiet window, reply, or frequency cap</td><td>Purchase, unsubscribe, audience change, or journey goal</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>Invalid consent, duplicate SMS, reply escalation, or contract overage</td><td>Stale audience, duplicate email, missing product event, or contact-cost jump</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Incremental mobile revenue, qualified reply, and compliant list growth</td><td>Email conversion, retained engagement, margin, and manageable operations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Final verdict</h2><p>Attentive wins when enterprise SMS depth and governance are the binding requirement. Mailchimp wins when email breadth, familiarity, and lower operating complexity matter more. Choose after both products pass a representative Shopify workflow and the twelve-month cost includes people, compliance, support, and suppression work.</p><p class="mt-6">Continue with the <a href="/blog">Shopify email guides</a>, <a href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase playbook</a>, and <a href="/alternatives">alternatives library</a>.</p>
    <section class="mt-12"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">FAQ</h2>{faqs.map(f=><details class="border-b border-border py-4"><summary class="font-semibold">{f.q}</summary><p class="mt-3 text-muted">{f.a}</p></details>)}</section>
  </div></article>
</BaseLayout>
